 \begin{abstract}
-For two opposite real linearly polarized plane waves, reciprocity fixes the extinction of a compact scatterer but permits different absorption and scattering. At a fixed vacuum wavenumber $k>0$, we establish that the supremum of the absorption difference divided by common extinction is one at every exactly prescribed extinction $E_*>0$. The realization uses finite compact two-layer local anisotropic nonmagnetic dielectrics satisfying arbitrary fixed upper caps $P>1$ and $Q>0$, with $I\preceq\ReH\varepsilon\preceq PI$ and $0\prec\ImH\varepsilon\preceq QI$ in each layer. Every individual body has asymmetry strictly below one. The constructive step is a physical aperture limit uniform after factorization by the squared sine of a material rotation angle, including its zero-angle auxiliary limit. This permits the coupling angle to decrease as the aperture increases while total extinction remains fixed. A subsequent positive isotropic-loss perturbation and a second intermediate-value argument preserve the exact target. Geometric resources are unrestricted across the approximating sequence, and no common positive loss floor or bandwidth is imposed. The polarization mechanism and reciprocal extinction identity have prior precedents; the contribution is the controlled compact Maxwell realization at prescribed extinction. A frozen Lean 4 formalization certifies the stated existence, suprema, and physical power identities, with the scope of its verification distinguished from independent semantic and priority review.
+Reciprocity equates the extinction cross sections for opposite real linearly polarized plane waves, while permitting different absorption and scattering. At a fixed vacuum wavenumber $k>0$, we prove that the supremum of the absorption difference divided by common extinction is one at every exactly prescribed extinction $E_*>0$. The construction uses finite compact two-layer local anisotropic nonmagnetic dielectrics satisfying arbitrary fixed caps $P>1$ and $Q>0$, with $I\preceq\ReH\varepsilon\preceq PI$ and $0\prec\ImH\varepsilon\preceq QI$ in each layer. Every individual body has asymmetry strictly below one. The key step is an aperture limit uniform after factoring out the squared sine of the material rotation angle, including its zero-angle auxiliary limit. This permits the coupling angle to decrease as the aperture grows while extinction remains fixed. Positive isotropic loss is then added, and continuity permits exact retuning to the target. The approximating sequence has no common geometric resource bound or positive loss floor, and no bandwidth guarantee is asserted. The contribution is the controlled compact Maxwell realization at prescribed extinction. An accompanying Lean 4 formalization verifies the main existence and supremum statements and physical power identities; its verification and review scope are specified separately.
 \end{abstract}
 
